\begin{abstract}
  Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) face challenges in processing long multimedia contexts due to the quadratic cost of self-attention and the linear growth of the Key-Value (KV) cache. Existing solutions largely compress context by reducing token count; however, at higher compression ratios, such approaches are difficult to keep near-lossless, motivating a new compression paradigm.
  We therefore introduce token dimension compression, an orthogonal paradigm that reduces the memory footprint of each token representation while preserving all input tokens and can be combined with token-number reduction.
  We instantiate this with M$^2$LA, transforming attention blocks in pretrained LLMs into Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA) ones. MLA's low-rank latent space (storing keys and values) enables compact token representations dynamically. It is training-light, involving RoPE-aware positional aggregation and modality-aware adapters for diverse multimodal data.
  M$^2$LA is a drop-in architectural change, maintaining near-lossless local context performance while achieving 2$\times$ KV-cache reductions and even slightly improving over the original model in various scenarios. This enables substantially longer multimodal contexts without sacrificing visual detail. 
\end{abstract}

