\subsection{Multimodal Video Understanding}
% \paragraph{Model Transfer Evaluation.}
Reparameterizing GQA to MLA reduces the per-token KV-cache dimensionality from 1024 to 530 while slightly improving overall accuracy. In Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, we evaluate overall short- and long-video capabilities under matched token budgets and observe that the reduced cache cost does not hurt performance, and can even improve it.
Further, we assess transfer on four representative tasks: short-video understanding and fine-grained perception(MVBench), multimodal knowledge and reasoning (VideoMMMU), temporal grounding (Charades-STA), and comprehensive long-video evaluation (VideoMME). As shown in Table~\ref{tab:loss_from_gqa_to_mla}, with nearly half the KV budget, VideoChat-Flash-{\method} can slightly \textbf{improve} the VideoMME average score and also achieves gains on the other three datasets. We attribute this improvement to reinvesting the saved KV budget into larger query/key head dimensions, increasing them from 128 to 256, which substantially enhances the expressive capacity of each head; this observation is also consistent with prior findings that larger head dimensions can improve \textbf{model expressivity}~\cite{Step325}. Together, these results indicate that the compressed KV representation not only preserves but can even strengthen spatiotemporal fidelity under fixed token budgets.









\subsection{Efficiency.} 
M$^2$LA significantly enhances the model's context capacity by reducing cache consumption. This optimization enables substantial scaling: during training, the model can process over 2,000 frames using 32 NVIDIA A100-80G GPUs with DeepSpeed ZeRO-3. For inference, a single NVIDIA A100-80G GPU is capable of handling more than 15,000 frames. We further quantify the per-frame KV-cache overhead with a simple metric below.

\paragraph{End-to-end Throughput.}
Beyond KV-cache footprint, we evaluate end-to-end inference efficiency. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:throughput_comparison}, M$^2$LA consistently improves decoding throughput over the original model across a wide range of prefill lengths, from 8k to 128k tokens, yielding approximately $1.3\times$--$1.8\times$ speedups. This improvement becomes more pronounced in longer-context regimes, where KV-cache related memory I/O increasingly dominates runtime. By reducing the KV-cache by $\sim$2$\times$, M$^2$LA alleviates this bottleneck and translates memory savings into practical latency and throughput gains. Results are measured on a single NVIDIA H200 GPU with batch size 1, greedy decoding, bf16 precision, and FlashAttention enabled, with each point averaged over 10 runs after warm-up.

