\paragraph{Metric: Cache Per Frame (CPF)}
To evaluate the memory efficiency of Video-LLMs during streaming, we propose a metric termed \textbf{Cache Per Frame (CPF)}. CPF quantifies the incremental memory footprint occupied by the Key-Value (KV) cache for each processed video frame per network layer. Formally, CPF is defined as:

\begin{equation}
    \text{CPF} = N_{\text{token}} \times D_{\text{kv}} \times S_{\text{prec}}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $N_{\text{token}}$ denotes the number of tokens used to represent a single video frame, $D_{\text{kv}}$ represents the dimensionality of the KV cache heads per layer, and $S_{\text{prec}}$ is the storage size (in bytes) determined by the model's precision (e.g., 2 bytes for BF16).

\paragraph{Efficiency Analysis.}
In our proposed architecture, the visual encoder and projector are optimized to compress each frame into merely $N_{\text{token}}=16$. Given the model configuration where the KV dimensionality $D_{\text{kv}}=530$ (resulting in $D_{\text{kv}} \times S_{\text{prec}} = 1,060$ bytes under BF16), we calculate the CPF as follows:

\begin{equation}
    \text{CPF}_{\text{Ours}} = 16 \times 1,060 \text{ bytes} \approx \mathbf{16.6 \text{ KB}}
\end{equation}

This metric highlights the extreme compactness of our method. In comparison, Qwen2.5-VL-7B exhibits a significantly higher memory cost with a CPF of approximately \textbf{3,848 KB}. Thus, our method reduces the per-frame cache overhead by a factor of \textbf{$\sim230\times$}, effectively eliminating the memory bottleneck for long-context video understanding. More results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:cpf_comparison}.
