% \subsection{Limitation}
% A potential limitation of the given {\method} lies in its reliance on a calibration phase for reparameterizing attention blocks into MLA. The effectiveness of RoPE aggregation and least-squares factorization for non-positional channels is directly tied to the used calibration dataset. If such data does not adequately capture visual and textual patterns during inference, the resulting latent space might not be optimally expressive, potentially leading to performance degradation on out-of-distribution scenarios. Furthermore, while preserving all input tokens, the compression of each token's representation into a latent space, by definition, involves information reduction. Though empirical results show near-lossless performance on current benchmarks, the extent to which extremely fine-grained visual or textual details are preserved under high compression ratios might vary, posing challenges for tasks demanding highly nuanced distinctions not fully covered in the current evaluation.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We present M$^2$LA, a training-light reparameterization that converts GQA blocks into Multimodel Multi-head Latent Attention for multimodal large models to address the challenge of processing long multimedia contexts. By compressing keys and values into a fixed-size latent space while sharing a compact RoPE component, M$^2$LA decouples KV-cache size from the number of visual tokens, preserving fine-grained spatiotemporal details under long contexts. 
We further introduced modality-aware adapters, frame prompts that create high-salience textual anchors, and a dynamic latent budgeting strategy. Experiments on both show and long video benchmarks show near-lossless short-context accuracy, strong long-context scaling, and favorable memory--compute trade-offs. We believe representation compression offers a simple and general path to scale multimodal context capacity without sacrificing visual fidelity, while remaining orthogonal to token-number reduction and thus readily composable with it. Future work will explore more sophisticated dynamic latent rank adjustment, end-to-end learning of compression matrices, and extending {\method}'s principles to other efficient attention mechanisms for even greater memory and computational efficiencies.


























































